<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Zachlumia</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1326 CE (approx.) · capital Ston</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Medieval principality of Hum in Herzegovina and southern Dalmatia, home of the Miroslav Gospel and contested by Serbia, Croatia and Bosnia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hum emerged among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> in the 7th to 9th centuries. Its best-known early ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihajlo-visevic">Mihajlo Višević</a>, governed in the early 10th century, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> and attending church councils at Split.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Princes ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a> and Bona near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blagaj">Blagaj</a>, over a population divided between Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches. Hum often fell under neighbours: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-principality-of-serbia">Grand Principality of Serbia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miroslav-of-hum">Prince Miroslav</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>, ruled it in the late 12th century and commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miroslav-gospel">Miroslav Gospel</a>, a masterpiece of Cyrillic illumination.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1326 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-ii-kotromanic">Stephen II Kotromanić</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banate-of-bosnia">Banate of Bosnia</a> took most of Hum, and in 1333 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peljesac">Pelješac</a> were sold to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa</a>. The region later became the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zachlumia.jpg" alt="Zachlumia" width="320" height="223"><figcaption>Image: PANONIAN; derivative work by Ceha · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paganija,_Zahumlje,_Travunija,_Duklja,_Croatian_view.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1326 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ston</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mihajlo Višević, Miroslav</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Catholic and Orthodox)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Serbian/Croatian (Slavonic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hum, Principality of Hum, Zahumlje</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1063&amp;focus=zachlumia" aria-label="Zachlumia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5108 656 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zachlumia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5428 912L5653 895M5428 912L5492 903M5428 912L5521 939M5428 912L5437 871"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5653 895h0M5492 903h0M5521 939h0M5437 871h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5428 912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5428 912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ston. <a href="/atlas?year=1063&amp;focus=zachlumia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

